### Reliable CSNP Exchange & Optimized LSP Entry TLV
- **Presentation**: [06-Reliable SNP](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-lsr-06-reliable-snp-00)
- **Presenter**: Les Ginsberg

Les Ginsberg presented two enhancements for IS-IS database synchronization over point-to-point (P2P) links:
1. **Reliable CSNP Exchange**: Introduces a CSNP Entry TLV carried in PSNPs to acknowledge contiguous ranges of received system IDs and explicitly signal missing gaps. Holds back adjacency advertisement until database synchronization is confirmed.
2. **Optimized LSP Entry TLV**: Reduces SNP entry size from 16 bytes to 8 bytes per LSP for multi-LSP nodes by omitting the lifetime field and using a checksum value of zero to signal purges (lifetime = 0).

- **Discussion**:
  - Tony Przygienda raised technical concerns regarding gap reporting vs. max ranges, safety across link flaps/restarts, interaction with RFC 8706 Graceful Restart (which relies on lifetime tracking), and noted that open-source implementations compute valid checksums on purges.
  - Christian Hopps and Les Ginsberg clarified that ISO 10589 states payload checksums are ignored on purged LSPs; in the new SNP entry TLV format, encoding a zero in the entry checksum field simply signals a zero lifetime across the wire without modifying payload processing. Les agreed to clarify the draft text.

