### Abstract Set Hashing (ASH) Update & ASH Graceful Restart
- **Presentation**: [08-ASH Update/ASH GR](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-lsr-ash-updateash-gr-00)
- **Presenter**: Tony Przygienda

Tony Przygienda presented implementation results and protocol updates for Abstract Set Hashing (ASH):
- **Performance Results**: Implementation tests showed a ~40% reduction in adjacency bring-up time, 70% CPU savings, and a 98–99% reduction in flooding/CSNP packet volume during link flaps on large link-state databases.
- **Protocol Updates**: Added a 1-byte population count to hash ranges, introduced a cache gate mechanism to prevent patch amplification, and migrated capability signaling to the IS-IS Hello Capability TLV.
- **Scalable Graceful Restart (SGR / ASH GR)**: Proposed a distributed Graceful Restart architecture where helper nodes freeze database snapshots locally and send hashes, allowing the restarter to pull only necessary data and avoid control-plane flooding meltdowns.
- **Discussion**:
  - Les Ginsberg questioned the necessity of separate Tx/Rx capability bits and advocated reusing existing CSNPs rather than defining new PDUs.
  - Tony Przygienda defended the dedicated PDUs and separate Tx/Rx capability flags.
  - The author requested Working Group adoption for ASH.

